A||N||1||Seanh||Paint repair?||||1||
Z||000000||Seanh||06-16-2001||04:20 PM||||Ok, I am planning on rattle can repainting some of my 86GT peices. The ground FX and all horrible, and all will be repained completely I think. The front bumper will also be completely repainted for sure. Now, the rear bumber is not that bad. Someone hit me while they were backing up in the parking lot. I wasn't there, so no idea who it was, so no money for me. Anyway, its just chipped up on the corner of the drivers side. The rest of the bumper is in good condition. So, how can I just repair this paint? <P>My dad suggests I lightly sand down the near surrounding area, heavily sanding the damaged area to make it a little more smooth as opposed to a "step" in paint layers. Then mask off the rest of the bumper and lightly apply a coat of paint, light on the area, heavy in the middle where the damge is. Then he said mask off more, leaveing almost just the damage spot and very little extra area, and paint a couple more light coats. Then remove all masking and sand smooth, then clear coat (probally the whole bumper right?) <P>So is that pretty much the way to do it? Since the cars undergoing repairs to the steering system from the crash, it will be undrivable for a couple days so this would be a good time to work on the paint also. Any reccomened brands? We have Advance Auto and wally world here, so whatever they carry will probally be what I'll use. Thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000001||theogre||06-16-2001||09:51 PM||||You want to have a smooth transition from new paint to old.... So if you are fixing a spot on the hood, you would mask well away from the chip. The idea is just to keep overspray off the car. You don't sray up to the mask or you'll have a line in the paint. <P>If the chip is near the edge of the hood you would mask everything on the fender but leave a big work area on the hood.<P>On the ground effects, obviously you'd mask everything else.... but leave a large area open on the trim so you can feather the paint.<P>For sanding.... nothing more coarse than 120-220 grit. Finer is better. You want to feather out the chips, not dig holes and deep scratches. Remember, the panels you are working on are not much harder than the paint.||||reg||1
